An abstract n-polytope P is a partially ordered set of "faces" satisfying the standard axioms (graded by rank from -1 to n, the existence of unique minimum and maximum faces, the diamond condition, and strong flag connectivity). A flag of P is a maximal chain of faces. The automorphism group Γ(P) acts on the set of flags, and P is called an m-orbit polytope when this action has exactly m orbits.

A realization of P is, broadly, a mapping that assigns to each face of P a geometric object in some Euclidean space E^d so that incidence is preserved, together with an associated symmetry group G ⊆ Isom(E^d). The realization is said to have k flag orbits if G acts on the (geometric) flags of the realized object with exactly k orbits. In general, the number k of flag orbits of the geometric realization may differ from the number m of flag orbits of the underlying abstract polytope, because symmetries of the abstract polytope need not be realizable as Euclidean isometries, and conversely a less symmetric abstract polytope may admit a more symmetric geometric placement.

Problem. Develop a general theory of k-orbit realizations of m-orbit abstract polytopes. In particular, formulate a rigorous framework that:
(i) defines precisely what it means for a realization of an m-orbit abstract polytope in Euclidean space to be a k-orbit realization (for arbitrary positive integers k and m, with k ≠ m permitted);
(ii) characterizes, for given m and k, which m-orbit abstract n-polytopes admit a k-orbit realization in some Euclidean space, and in which dimensions;
(iii) describes the corresponding realization spaces and their structure (e.g., dimension, connectedness, action of the automorphism group), extending the existing theory for regular (1-orbit) and 2-orbit cases;
(iv) relates the symmetry type graph of the realized object to the symmetry type graph of the underlying abstract polytope.

